WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.990
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.410
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:15.692
Unit sixteen: Happiness and Wellbeing. In this unit, you will explore what makes a good and happy life.

4
00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.137
subjective wellbeing.

5
00:00:18.837 --> 00:00:23.310
subjective wellbeing means how happy and satisfied people say they feel.

6
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.863
Surveys measure subjective wellbeing across countries.

7
00:00:27.563 --> 00:00:31.144
Your turn. Say: subjective wellbeing.

8
00:00:33.844 --> 00:00:35.287
subjective wellbeing.

9
00:00:36.283 --> 00:00:37.865
life satisfaction.

10
00:00:38.565 --> 00:00:43.014
life satisfaction means how good people believe their life is overall.

11
00:00:43.314 --> 00:00:47.996
Life satisfaction is higher in countries with strong communities.

12
00:00:48.696 --> 00:00:51.952
Your turn. Say: life satisfaction.

13
00:00:54.652 --> 00:00:56.198
life satisfaction.

14
00:00:57.183 --> 00:00:58.184
hedonic.

15
00:00:58.884 --> 00:01:01.743
hedonic means related to pleasure and enjoyment.

16
00:01:02.043 --> 00:01:04.612
Eating ice cream gives hedonic pleasure.

17
00:01:05.312 --> 00:01:08.742
Your turn. Say: hedonic.

18
00:01:11.442 --> 00:01:12.327
hedonic.

19
00:01:13.317 --> 00:01:14.620
eudaimonia.

20
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:21.557
eudaimonia means a deep happiness that comes from living with purpose and virtue.

21
00:01:21.857 --> 00:01:26.434
Aristotle believed eudaimonia was the highest human good.

22
00:01:27.134 --> 00:01:29.856
Your turn. Say: eudaimonia.

23
00:01:32.556 --> 00:01:33.731
eudaimonia.

24
00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:35.671
flourishing.

25
00:01:36.371 --> 00:01:40.403
flourishing means growing and living in a healthy, successful way.

26
00:01:40.703 --> 00:01:43.503
Education is essential for human flourishing.

27
00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:47.146
Your turn. Say: flourishing.

28
00:01:49.846 --> 00:01:50.754
flourishing.

29
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:52.693
contentment.

30
00:01:53.393 --> 00:01:56.786
contentment means a calm feeling of being satisfied.

31
00:01:57.086 --> 00:02:00.235
My grandmother lives a simple life full of contentment.

32
00:02:00.935 --> 00:02:03.645
Your turn. Say: contentment.

33
00:02:06.345 --> 00:02:07.346
contentment.

34
00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.477
positive psychology.

35
00:02:11.177 --> 00:02:16.358
positive psychology means the study of strengths and what makes life worth living.

36
00:02:16.658 --> 00:02:19.784
Positive psychology studies gratitude and hope.

37
00:02:20.484 --> 00:02:23.833
Your turn. Say: positive psychology.

38
00:02:26.533 --> 00:02:28.300
positive psychology.

39
00:02:29.283 --> 00:02:30.760
social connection.

40
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:35.573
social connection means meaningful relationships with other people.

41
00:02:35.873 --> 00:02:39.277
Social connection is the strongest predictor of happiness.

42
00:02:39.977 --> 00:02:42.920
Your turn. Say: social connection.

43
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.062
social connection.

44
00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.340
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.230
Ms. Sarah, would you be happier now if you had stayed in America?

46
00:02:55.517 --> 00:03:01.048
Not necessarily. If I hadn't come to Cambodia, I wouldn't have found work I love.

47
00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.582
So purpose matters more than pleasure?

48
00:03:05.883 --> 00:03:11.412
Psychologists distinguish hedonic happiness from eudaimonia, a deeper sense of meaning.

49
00:03:12.717 --> 00:03:17.027
My grandfather has very little money, yet he seems more content than anyone.

50
00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:24.940
That's typical. Research shows social connection matters more than income above a certain level.

51
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.679
If people knew that, would they work less?

52
00:03:29.983 --> 00:03:34.575
Perhaps. If I were younger, I'd certainly spend more time with friends.

53
00:03:35.883 --> 00:03:39.323
Grammar focus: Mixed conditionals (advanced).

54
00:03:39.623 --> 00:03:42.272
Mixed conditionals connect different times.

55
00:03:42.572 --> 00:03:45.965
If I hadn't come to Cambodia, I wouldn't be so happy now.

56
00:03:46.265 --> 00:03:49.623
If she were less ambitious, she wouldn't have moved abroad.

57
00:03:49.923 --> 00:03:56.915
Mixed conditionals let you link imagined pasts and presents precisely, which is useful for reflection and argument.

58
00:03:57.917 --> 00:04:00.929
Now read and listen. What Really Makes Us Happy?.

59
00:04:01.229 --> 00:04:05.167
For decades, governments measured success mainly through economic growth.

60
00:04:06.483 --> 00:04:11.443
Today, many also measure subjective wellbeing and life satisfaction.

61
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:19.289
Research suggests that, above a certain income, more money adds surprisingly little happiness.

62
00:04:20.583 --> 00:04:25.567
Instead, social connection, purpose and health appear to matter most.

63
00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:32.402
Philosophers distinguish hedonic pleasure from eudaimonia, a deeper kind of fulfilment.

64
00:04:33.717 --> 00:04:38.990
Positive psychology encourages habits such as gratitude, kindness and mindful attention.

65
00:04:40.283 --> 00:04:45.092
Bhutan famously measures Gross National Happiness alongside economic data.

66
00:04:46.383 --> 00:04:51.262
Perhaps flourishing, rather than wealth, should be the true goal of society.

67
00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.388
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:55.688 --> 00:04:58.616
A deep happiness from living with purpose is blank.

69
00:04:58.916 --> 00:05:02.941
A, eudaimonia. B, hedonic. C, contentment.

70
00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.414
The answer is A: eudaimonia.

71
00:05:10.917 --> 00:05:13.729
If I had studied medicine, I blank a doctor now.

72
00:05:14.029 --> 00:05:18.240
A, would be. B, would have been. C, will be.

73
00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.760
The answer is A: would be.

74
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:29.794
If he were more careful, he blank the accident yesterday.

75
00:05:30.094 --> 00:05:33.817
A, wouldn't have had. B, wouldn't have. C, won't have.

76
00:05:37.817 --> 00:05:40.106
The answer is A: wouldn't have had.

77
00:05:41.617 --> 00:05:44.812
Meaningful relationships with others are blank.

78
00:05:45.112 --> 00:05:52.365
A, social connection. B, life satisfaction. C, positive psychology.

79
00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:59.151
The answer is A: social connection.

80
00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:05.053
According to the text, which country measures Gross National Happiness?

81
00:06:05.353 --> 00:06:09.134
A, Japan. B, Bhutan. C, Cambodia.

82
00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:14.964
The answer is B: Bhutan.

83
00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:21.390
Now it's your turn. Part one: What is the secret of a happy life?

84
00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:33.031
Part two: Use a mixed conditional, give two factors, and support them with an example.

85
00:06:39.331 --> 00:06:49.370
I'd say purpose and relationships. If I hadn't joined my volunteer group, I wouldn't feel so connected to my community now. Pleasure fades quickly, but meaning lasts.

86
00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.565
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:57.617
Great work today! You learned eight new words and mixed conditionals (advanced).

88
00:06:57.917 --> 00:07:01.510
Today's value is kindness. Be kind to everyone.

89
00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.486
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:04.786 --> 00:07:07.716
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

